{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013iaskJ4GttvNPrDvvkdJTA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully and propose the next task.\n\n## Step 1: Analyze Project Context\n\nThis is a specification-driven development project that regenerates Unix utilities in Go. The pipeline is: extract specs from C source → specify (PRD + use case) → synthesize (AI generates Go code) → verify (differential testing against GNU reference binaries).\n\n## Step 2: Summarize Project State\n\n**Problem:** Classic Unix utilities lack formal specifications. Re-implementations diverge from reference behavior on edge cases. This project derives specs from C source and generates Go implementations verified by differential testing.\n\n**Architecture:**\n- `cmd/` - One package per utility (main.go + tests)\n\n\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n- `pkg/sys/` - Platform-specific syscall abstractions\n- `pkg/format/` - Output formatting (columns, colors, sizes)\n- `magefiles/` - Build system\n- `docs/` - Specs, PRDs, use cases, test suites\n\n**Current state:** The differential testing harness and platform-specific syscall abstractions are already implemented, with fileinfo handling for both Darwin and Linux systems in place.\n\nThe core formatting utilities for colors, columns, and sizes are complete, along with the main command implementations for cat, wc, sponge, and version—each with their corresponding test coverage.\n\n**Releases in scope:** Looking at the release structure from 00.0 through 01.3, starting with the foundational infrastructure batch.\n\nNow I'm reviewing the status across all planned releases—the specifications are finalized but implementation hasn't begun yet for the format utilities, system utilities, test utilities, and the individual command batches for wc, cat, sponge, and the remaining work in 01.3.\n\nWait, I need to check the actual source code that exists. Looking at what's already implemented: testutils has difftest utilities, sys has file info and terminal handling across platforms, format has color and column utilities, and cat already has a main command. The code status says \"not started\" but the source code field shows these implementations already exist, so I should trust the actual code over the status descriptions and not propose reimplementing what's already there.\n\nThe releases 00.0 through 01.1 are essentially complete based on the code that's present. What's actually missing is the test file for sponge and the entire du command implementation, though du's specification is already done. Release 01.2 - sponge. Source code shows cmd/sponge/main.go but NO sponge_test.go. The tests are missing!\n\nSo the highest priority incomplete work is: **cmd/sponge/ differential tests** (rel01.2).\n\nThe sponge test file needs to be created following the same differential testing pattern as cat_test.go and wc_test.go, covering the five requirements from prd007-sponge.\n\nNow I'm figuring out what the reference binary should be - moreutils provides `sponge` directly without the g-prefix that Homebrew uses for GNU coreutils, so I need to determine the correct reference binary name for the test setup.\n\nLooking at the PRD requirements, I need to cover stdin absorption with configurable buffering and temp directories, atomic file output with permission preservation, append mode behavior, passthrough mode for streaming, and comprehensive error handling across all these scenarios.\n\nFor the differential tests, I'll need to validate basic stdin-to-file operations, append mode with the -a flag, passthrough mode when no file is specified, error cases, and mixed scenarios. Since sponge writes to files rather than stdout, I'll use the ExpectedFiles map in the DiffTest struct to compare the actual output files between both binaries instead of just comparing stdout.\n\nThe challenge is that running both binaries in the same working directory would cause them to overwrite each other's output files, so I need to execute them in separate directories and then compare the resulting files to ensure they match. I see the issue now—the ExpectedFiles field contains hardcoded expected content, so it's checking what the Go binary wrote (since it ran second and overwrote the reference output). This means I can't reliably compare file outputs between the two binaries using the current RunDiffTests structure. I need to either run each binary in separate directories or implement a custom comparison that captures both outputs before they overwrite each other.\n\nFor the sponge test file, I should focus on passthrough mode tests that write to stdout—those work fine with RunDiffTests since both binaries can output without interfering. The file-output mode would need separate test logic, but given the constraints, I'll prioritize the passthrough tests that demonstrate stdin absorption and the core functionality. Looking at the requirements breakdown, I can see the five main areas: stdin absorption, file output, append mode, passthrough mode, and error handling. For the test task with the 4-requirement limit, I should focus on the most testable ones—passthrough mode works well with RunDiffTests for stdout comparison, while file output and append mode need custom verification logic, and error handling rounds out the coverage.\n\nFor the implementation, I'm thinking through how sponge behaves across different modes: it absorbs stdin and either writes to a file or stdout depending on arguments, with append mode toggled by the -a flag. Passthrough mode is straightforward since both binaries output to stdout, but file-output mode requires running the reference binary first to capture what the output file should contain, then comparing against the test binary's result.\n\nI'm settling on writing custom differential test functions that run each binary in isolated temp directories and compare the resulting files—this approach is cleaner than trying to manage expected files. The test structure should follow the existing patterns from cat_test.go and wc_test.go, with TestMain setup and RunDiffTests for stdout comparisons, while the file-output tests use the custom comparison logic.\n\nFor the reference binary, moreutils installs sponge without a g-prefix on Homebrew, so I'd look it up with `exec.LookPath(\"sponge\")`. The tricky part is making sure the test finds the Homebrew version and not our Go binary—but since we build our binary to a temp directory outside the PATH, LookPath should correctly find the Homebrew sponge as long as it's installed. I'm sketching out the test structure for sponge's file-output mode, which requires a helper function that sets up identical temp directories, runs both the reference and Go implementations with the same inputs, then compares their outputs and exit codes. I'm estimating around 210 lines total across TestMain, helper functions, and test cases for passthrough, file output, append, and error handling modes.\n\nNow I need to write a comprehensive task description for the stitch agent that covers the testing pattern from existing files, the reference binary name, the DiffTest struct API, how to handle file-output differential testing, and what sponge actually does. I should also check which PRD to reference—likely the sponge PRD based on the naming convention.\n\nLooking at what the stitch agent needs to read: the sponge implementation, the differential testing harness, an example test file like cat_test.go to follow the pattern, and the test suite spec for sponge which should exist at the release-specific path since the spec status shows it's complete.\n\nNow I'm finalizing the task proposal—since I can only suggest one task and sponge tests are the highest priority incomplete work for rel01.2, I'll draft that as the task with the required reading materials and deliverable type specified.\n\nWriting test requirements... I need to locate the sponge binary using exec.LookPath and skip tests if it's not found, similar to how cat_test.go handles missing reference binaries. For passthrough mode I can use the standard diff test utilities since both binaries write to stdout, but for file output and append modes I'll need a helper that runs each binary in separate temporary directories with identical setup, then compares the output files byte-for-byte along with stderr and exit codes. The test file should be created at cmd/sponge/sponge_test.go with these differential test cases covering basic functionality, append mode, and error handling. I'm verifying the file naming convention matches the existing test pattern of `<utility>_test.go`, so this should be `sponge_test.go`. I'm also confirming that `sponge` is the correct reference binary name from moreutils—Homebrew installs it without the g-prefix since there's no naming conflict on macOS like there is with GNU coreutils. Let me make sure I'm proposing the right task by reviewing what's already been completed. source_code:\n- pkg/testutils/ - has difftest.go AND difftest_test.go ✓\n- pkg/sys/ - has fileinfo.go, fileinfo_darwin.go, fileinfo_linux.go, terminal.go AND fileinfo_test.go, terminal_test.go ✓\n- pkg/format/ - has color.go, column.go, size.go AND color_test.go, column_test.go, size_test.go ✓\n- cmd/cat/ - has main.go AND cat_test.go ✓\n- cmd/wc/ - has main.go AND wc_test.go ✓\n- cmd/sponge/ - has main.go but NO sponge_test.go ✗\n- cmd/version/ - has main.go AND version_test.go ✓\n\nThe sponge test file is the next priority for rel01.2, followed by the entire cmd/du/ implementation for rel01.3. I'm checking my constraints: exactly 4 requirements fits the limit, and the test file should land within the 150-250 line target even though it's test code rather than production code.\n\nMy task title includes the PRD requirement IDs as specified, and I need to add trace annotations matching the pattern used in existing tests—something like `test-rel01.2` and `rel01.2-uc001-sponge` so the stitch agent captures them correctly.\n\nNow I'm mapping out the test file structure, following the same organization as cat_test.go: package setup and imports, binary path variables, a TestMain function to locate the reference sponge binary and build the Go version, then helper functions for file operations and differential testing, followed by test functions covering passthrough mode, basic file output, empty input handling, and the main application behavior.\n\nI need to add tests for the append flag with both existing and new files, plus error handling for unwritable directories—this should bring the whole test file to around 180-220 lines, which feels right.\n\nNow I'm finalizing the YAML output.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":71935,"cache_read_input_tokens":19292,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":71935},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a9651dae-571f-44b3-827f-4096b0ed1d2e","uuid":"f968414a-7ec0-4f31-9a44-ef92447a52ff"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013iaskJ4GttvNPrDvvkdJTA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem:** Classic Unix utilities lack formal specifications. This project derives specs from C source, generates Go implementations via AI, and verifies parity through differential testing against GNU reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture:** `cmd/` (one package per utility), `pkg/testutils/` (differential test harness), `pkg/sys/` (platform syscalls), `pkg/format/` (output formatting), `magefiles/` (build system), `docs/` (specs).\n\n**Implementation status (from source_code):**\n- **Complete:** pkg/testutils, pkg/sys, pkg/format, cmd/version, cmd/wc, cmd/cat, cmd/sponge (implementation only)\n- **Missing:** cmd/sponge/sponge_test.go (rel01.2), cmd/du (rel01.3)\n\n**Current priority:** Release 01.2 (sponge) is the earliest incomplete release — `main.go` exists but differential tests are missing.\n\n## Priorities\n\nReleases 00.0, 01.0, 01.1 are complete (code exists in source_code). Release 01.2 has `cmd/sponge/main.go` but no `sponge_test.go`. This is the next task.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"prd007-sponge R1-R5: differential tests for cmd/sponge\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd007-sponge.yaml (sponge requirements R1-R5)\n      - docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-01.2.yaml (test suite specification for sponge)\n      - cmd/sponge/main.go (implementation under test -- read to understand all flags and behaviors)\n      - pkg/testutils/difftest.go (DiffTest struct, RunDiffTests, NormalizeFunc, ExpectedFiles)\n      - cmd/cat/cat_test.go (reference pattern for TestMain, writeFile helper, and RunDiffTests usage)\n      - cmd/wc/wc_test.go (reference pattern for differential test structure)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/sponge/sponge_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: Differential tests comparing Go sponge binary against moreutils reference sponge binary\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Implement differential tests for passthrough mode (prd007-sponge R4): when sponge is invoked with no output file argument, it reads all of stdin and writes to stdout. Use testutils.RunDiffTests to compare stdout, stderr, and exit code against the reference binary. Test with non-empty stdin and empty stdin.\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Implement differential tests for file output mode (prd007-sponge R1, R2): sponge reads all of stdin then writes to a named output file via atomic rename. Since both binaries write to files rather than stdout, run each binary in a separate temporary directory with identical pre-conditions (same stdin bytes, same pre-existing files, same arguments), then compare the resulting output file contents byte-for-byte along with stderr and exit code.\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Implement differential tests for append mode (prd007-sponge R3): the -a flag causes sponge to append stdin content after any existing file content. Test appending to an existing file with content and appending to a non-existent file (which should behave like default write mode).\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Implement differential tests for error handling (prd007-sponge R5): verify that error messages on stderr and exit codes match the reference binary when given invalid conditions such as an output file in a non-existent directory.\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Use exec.LookPath(\\\"sponge\\\") to locate the Homebrew moreutils reference binary. Moreutils tools are installed without a g-prefix (unlike GNU coreutils which use gcat, gwc, etc.). If the reference binary is not found, print a skip message and call os.Exit(0), matching the pattern in cmd/cat/cat_test.go and cmd/wc/wc_test.go.\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"For passthrough mode tests (no output file argument), use testutils.RunDiffTests directly since both binaries write to stdout and the standard stdout/stderr/exit-code comparison applies.\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"For file-output and append-mode tests, write a helper function (e.g., runFileDiffTest) that creates two separate temporary directories, sets up identical pre-existing files in each, runs the reference binary in one directory and the Go binary in the other with the same stdin and arguments, then compares the output file contents byte-for-byte and reports divergences in stderr and exit code using t.Errorf. This avoids the problem of both binaries overwriting the same output file in a shared WorkDir.\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"cmd/sponge/sponge_test.go exists, is in package main, and compiles with no errors via go build ./cmd/sponge/.\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"go test ./cmd/sponge/ passes when the reference sponge binary is available (Homebrew moreutils installed).\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"Tests cover all four modes: passthrough (no file argument), file output (named file argument), append (-a flag), and at least one error case (non-existent parent directory).\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"File header includes copyright, SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT, Implements tracing to prd007-sponge requirements, and Traces to test-rel01.2 and rel01.2-uc001-sponge.\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":71935,"cache_read_input_tokens":19292,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":71935},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a9651dae-571f-44b3-827f-4096b0ed1d2e","uuid":"086a48c6-a3d4-4c63-b125-92d6e2fc886a"}
